WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:06.520
Kellogg, Idaho is really more of a&nbsp;
car dealership than it is a town.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:16.800
Its population is 2,255. Dave Smith Motors,&nbsp;
the local dealership, employs about 400.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:23.040
On any given day, more cars may sit on its&nbsp;
lot than there are people who live in Kellogg.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.040
And make no mistake — these are not&nbsp;
second-hand 30-year-old beaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:31.600
left to rust in the middle-of-nowhere-Idaho.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:38.080
No, these are brand new seventy, eighty, one&nbsp;
hundred thousand dollar Ford Super Duties — in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:46.160
a place where the median household income is&nbsp;
$35,000 a year — 30% less than Mississippi.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:53.680
Neither is Kellogg really near anything. Just&nbsp;
12,000 people live within a 20-mile radius.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:01:00.240
The closest major airport is in Spokane,&nbsp;
Washington — over an hour’s drive away.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.160
…And yet this tiny, rural,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:08.320
and remote pitstop on I-90 is home to&nbsp;
the nation’s largest Jeep Ram dealer.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.400
It’s quite surreal.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.520
As you drive through Kellogg — which takes all of&nbsp;
about two and a half minutes — you’ll see a diner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:22.440
a post office, and a hardware store — the&nbsp;
things you’d expect in a town of 2,000 people.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:25.600
This shack doubles as its&nbsp;
city hall, police station,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:32.680
and zoning department. This, by the&nbsp;
way, is its mayor — now 81 years old.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.560
Then, out of nowhere, you’ll find&nbsp;
yourself surrounded by row after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:43.360
row of pristine Chrysler, Dodge,&nbsp;
Jeep, Ram, Chevy, and GMC trucks.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:49.280
Now, Kellogg may be an extreme example —&nbsp;
for reasons we’ll soon get into — but all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:55.560
across America are small towns like this&nbsp;
one overshadowed by giant dealerships.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.520
Their owners are often the richest people around.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:06.440
When we imagine the ultra-wealthy, we think of&nbsp;
celebrities, venture capitalists, and politicians.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:12.560
In reality, America’s .1% are hiding&nbsp;
in plain sight — they own law firms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.200
doctor’s offices, and car dealerships.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:22.200
Last year, the average Toyota dealer in&nbsp;
the Boston area made a $6 million profit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:28.800
And an estimated 20% of all owners make at&nbsp;
least a million and a half dollars a year.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.880
So, why is buying and reselling&nbsp;
a commodity — cars — as lucrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:37.480
as becoming an accomplished doctor or lawyer?

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.480
Because of something called&nbsp;
“Dealer Franchise Laws”.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:49.560
Whereas Apple, Sony, and Samsung can open their&nbsp;
own stores, Ford, Toyota, and Honda cannot.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:57.040
By law, cars must be sold by middlemen, who&nbsp;
often have exclusivity over their region.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.760
It wasn’t always this way.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:07.120
In 1949, the U.S. population was&nbsp;
just 45% of what it is today. Yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:13.040
there were three times as many car&nbsp;
dealerships — nearly 50,000 in total.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:17.960
Dealers, at the time, were scattered&nbsp;
throughout the country. They were small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:23.080
mom-and-pop operations that served&nbsp;
only their local communities.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:28.680
Carmakers, on the other hand, were far&nbsp;
more consolidated than they are today.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.920
Before the rise of foreign brands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:36.680
there were just the “Big Three” —&nbsp;
Ford, General Motors, and Chrysler.

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:40.200
This created an extreme imbalance of power.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.360
Since dealers depended on these&nbsp;
powerful corporations for inventory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.920
the latter effectively wielded total control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:54.280
Carmakers could offload risk onto dealers&nbsp;
by forcing them to buy unpopular models.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:04:00.840
So, although cars were initially sold many&nbsp;
different ways — in factory stores, by mail order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:07.720
and even by traveling salesmen — all 50 states&nbsp;
eventually stepped in to protect dealers.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:13.160
By, for example, making it illegal to force&nbsp;
them to buy cars they didn’t want. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:19.280
carmakers could no longer undercut their&nbsp;
prices by selling directly to consumers.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:22.560
But they didn’t stop there. These laws went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.840
further — shifting nearly all the&nbsp;
risk back onto the manufacturer.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.600
Apple, of course, can — at any time —&nbsp;
simply stop supplying Best Buy with iPhones.&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.360
But, in many states, carmakers can only terminate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:41.120
their franchise agreement for&nbsp;
specific reasons — like fraud.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:44.560
Otherwise, they must compensate&nbsp;
the dealer — for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:50.520
the value of all future profits,&nbsp;
future rent, and unsold inventory.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:53.840
If dealers previously shouldered all the risk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:59.560
now they assumed zero. State law&nbsp;
virtually guaranteed their profitability.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:06.360
Needless to say, this made them very,&nbsp;
very comfortable. Especially as family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:12.520
dealerships were passed down to children,&nbsp;
who came to expect this special treatment.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.200
Then came the internet.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:20.360
Long insulated from competition,&nbsp;
dealers were paralyzed.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:24.440
And, in what would become a pattern,&nbsp;
they argued their very existence was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:29.720
threatened. Thus, they lobbied for&nbsp;
even more government protection.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:32.400
In reality, the internet wasn’t a threat to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.920
industry. It was only a threat&nbsp;
to lazy uncompetitive dealers.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:43.360
Like any shift in the market, it&nbsp;
simply created new winners and losers.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.640
Only this time the winners&nbsp;
often came from the smallest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:51.600
most remote parts of the&nbsp;
country — like Kellogg, Idaho.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:57.440
When Dave Smith Motors opened its doors&nbsp;
in 1965, Idaho was the silver capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:03.440
of the world. Miners flocked to the&nbsp;
region and sustained the local economy.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.960
Then, after a brief spike, the price of&nbsp;
silver came crashing down in the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:11.760
leaving thousands unemployed.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:15.600
Unlike most dealerships, therefore,&nbsp;
which had grown complacent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:21.160
Dave Smith was forced to adapt —&nbsp;
the local population had collapsed.

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:27.440
For these small-town dealers, the internet&nbsp;
presented an opportunity, not a threat.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.400
Thanks to Idaho’s cheap land and labor,&nbsp;
Dave Smith could beat larger dealers on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:40.000
price. Customers would shop online and then fly&nbsp;
from as far away as Alaska or Florida. When they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:46.040
landed in Spokane, a Dave Smith shuttle&nbsp;
bus drove them an hour East to Kellogg.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:49.240
These tiny, regional dealers slowly grew into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:55.600
unstoppable corporate behemoths — merging&nbsp;
and swallowing up hundreds of competitors.

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:02.320
Dave Smith, for instance, is today part of Sonic&nbsp;
Automotive — the nation’s fifth-largest dealer.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:08.080
Or, take the number one auto group in&nbsp;
the nation — Lithia Motors. Last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:18.520
Lithia sold 679,000 cars — 1,800 per day. In&nbsp;
2020, it acquired a new dealership every 5 days.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:25.240
And in the two years between 2020 and ‘22,&nbsp;
the company doubled its annual revenue. Yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:32.880
it all started in little Ashland, Oregon — a&nbsp;
quiet town of about 6,000 people at the time.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:38.160
Now, even a hundred years ago,&nbsp;
Dealer Franchise Laws were peculiar.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:43.720
Here was the government essentially&nbsp;
negotiating contracts on behalf of dealers.

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:48.960
Like laws in New Jersey and Oregon making it&nbsp;
illegal for drivers to pump their own gas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:54.280
these restrictions were more than a&nbsp;
little silly, arbitrary, and inefficient.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:08:00.600
Still, the intentions were defensible. These&nbsp;
laws kept the lights on for countless small-town&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:08.200
family dealerships. And besides, it was hard&nbsp;
to muster much sympathy for giants like Toyota.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:12.960
But today, the balance of&nbsp;
power has unmistakably shifted.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:17.240
Last year, Lithia Motors made $30 billion — more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:23.280
than the worldwide revenue of Subaru&nbsp;
or Mazda, and not far behind Volvo.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:30.040
The average dealership now generates $71 million&nbsp;
in sales each year. And the average price of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:38.000
new car increased by 40% between 2015 and&nbsp;
22 — driven largely by dealer mark-ups.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:44.480
In other words, the days of mom-and-pop&nbsp;
dealerships are well and truly over.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:47.760
So why do the laws of all 50 states continue to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:55.760
favor Lithia — a $30 billion corporation&nbsp;
— over Subaru — a $28 billion corporation?

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:01.640
Here’s a hint: virtually every state&nbsp;
legislator has a dealership in their district.

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:05.840
Dealers sponsor Little League&nbsp;
teams, donate to Meals on Wheels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:09.600
and keep local newspapers&nbsp;
alive through advertising.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:12.960
In Nevada and Arizona, the average dealer employs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:20.120
almost a hundred people. Car sales account&nbsp;
for about 20% of all state sales taxes. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:25.440
the National Dealers’ Association is&nbsp;
one the most powerful lobbying groups.

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:31.600
In short, dealers are a strong and united&nbsp;
political force to be reckoned with.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:34.600
Then came electric vehicles.

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:37.320
When Tesla entered the market in 2013,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:43.600
it decided not to sell through dealers,&nbsp;
who it argued were biased against EVs.

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:49.520
Dealers, unsurprisingly, make the vast&nbsp;
majority of their revenue from sales. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:54.560
you might argue they have no reason to&nbsp;
sell one make or model over another.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:59.800
In terms of profit, however, dealers&nbsp;
are mechanics who also sell cars on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:06.000
side. Historically, most make more&nbsp;
from parts and service than sales.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:09.160
And since electric vehicles require less service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:13.600
they generate about 40%&nbsp;
less “aftermarket revenue”.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:19.920
So, when Consumer Reports sent secret&nbsp;
shoppers to 85 dealerships asking about EVs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:27.160
35 advised them to buy conventional instead.&nbsp;
A few even pretended they didn’t sell them.

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:34.120
Simply put: Tesla couldn’t trust them. And&nbsp;
that put lawmakers in an awkward position.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:39.040
Previously, they could argue that dealers&nbsp;
needed government protection to survive.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:44.880
But Tesla didn’t have a relationship with dealers&nbsp;
to protect in the first place. Thus, they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:50.320
provide no clear reason why it shouldn’t&nbsp;
be allowed to sell directly to consumers.

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:53.280
Every state has responded differently.

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:58.760
In Michigan, Tesla can “market” its cars by&nbsp;
opening “showrooms” and offering test drives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:03.440
so long as the actual “sale”&nbsp;
technically occurs out of state.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:10.200
In Texas, its cars must first be exported out&nbsp;
of state and then re-imported to a local buyer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:17.120
Tesla salespeople can’t legally mention prices&nbsp;
or even direct buyers to the company’s website.

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:21.600
In Connecticut, Tesla drivers&nbsp;
can lease but not buy.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:29.040
And in New Mexico, the company opened dealerships&nbsp;
on tribal land, where most state laws don’t apply.

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:33.440
Other states make special exceptions&nbsp;
for Tesla — it can open, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:36.720
up to three dealerships in Ohio.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:43.320
Strange as these laws are, they’ve started drawing&nbsp;
attention to the shady practices of dealers.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:48.800
Slowly but surely, momentum is starting&nbsp;
to build in the opposite direction.

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:55.840
Ford and Volvo recently announced plans to start&nbsp;
selling their vehicles online. Slow and arduous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.840 --> 00:12:03.600
price negotiations are going out of style. And&nbsp;
Hyundai will soon even sell cars on Amazon.

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:09.480
After nearly a century, the dealer&nbsp;
monopoly is finally weakening.

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:15.320
Similarly, traditional media companies no longer&nbsp;
have a monopoly on great entertainment. Today’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:21.240
creators can bypass yesterday’s gatekeepers&nbsp;
and control our distribution — which is why we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:27.600
